WOW-BG3 benilla patch 0013: frame pacing (on top of 0001-0009; skips 0010).

A test on the desktop (BG3 fullscreen 3440x1440 at 60): Tav looked lower frame
rate than the world and the camera staggered. benilla drew on its own 60 Hz clock with the
newest BG3 camera record, Tav blended one BG3 frame behind on BG3's clock (0007), and the
capture layer took the newest WoW frame at each BG3 present: three clocks.

- wowbg3_pace.rs (new): frame packets. The capture layer appends the camera record it read at
  each BG3 present to Tav's pose file (tavpose.bin "TVC1" trailer, the last 128 bytes). While
  packets come, each frame waits at its start (First) for the next packet (at most 60 ms; none
  for 250 ms: benilla's own clock as before), so benilla draws exactly one frame per BG3 present
  with the camera and Tav's pose of that present. FrameTag (packet, camera seq, flags, depth
  offset) is extracted into the render world for the export. `wowbg3 pace` log lines every 600
  frames. WOW_BG3_PACE=off turns it off. Needs WOW_NOVSYNC=1 (benilla's own present must not
  hold it to 60 Hz; its window is never shown).
- wowbg3_lead.rs (follow_bg3 only): the camera record comes from the frame's packet when there is
  one, else from bg3cam.bin as before. Ground lock and clearance unchanged.
- wowbg3_tav.rs (apply_pose only): in lockstep the packet's pose is drawn as it is (no blending).
- wowbg3_frames.rs: non-blocking export. Three readback buffer pairs; each frame's copies are
  mapped without waiting and a writer thread polls the device and writes the slot file (the
  render thread used to wait for the frame's GPU work and the 40 MB write at 3440x1440 every
  frame). WBF1 bytes 36..56: packet u32, camera seq u32, flags u32 (bit 0 lockstep), publish
  time f64 (CLOCK_MONOTONIC s); the depth offset now comes with the frame (FrameTag) instead of
  the main world's current value. `wowbg3 frames` log lines every 10 s.

